From 956192e9749da2cdae085163e77d0927f24e3eec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JohnnyC96 <54557697+JohnnyC96@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 12:06:50 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Guest memory: runtime pools below 0x100000; SetupHeap(-1)
 ends at the stack

The runtime kept its RPC packet/server pools, TLS pool, boot-mode pool and
async callback stacks in 0x1F00000-0x2000000, and capped every guest heap at
0x1F00000. On hardware that RAM belongs to the game: with heap_size -1 the
heap ends at the thread's stack pointer (ps2tek, SetupHeap), and ps2sdk's
crt0 puts a 64-128 KB stack at the top of RAM. The callback-stack floor
(0x1F00000) also overlapped the RPC packet pool.

Now the runtime's own guest memory lives in the EE kernel's area between its
syscall-table mirror and the ELF load address (PS2_RUNTIME_RESERVED_BASE
0x80000 .. PS2_RUNTIME_RESERVED_END 0x100000): pools at 0x80000-0xB1000,
callback stacks from 0x100000 down to 0xB1000 (19 stacks of 16 KB, cached
per thread and depth). SetupHeap(base, -1) ends the heap at the stack pointer
SetupThread recorded; an explicit size is honoured up to the end of RAM.
Before SetupHeap the heap limit stays 0x1F00000.

Found with Resident Evil Outbreak (SLPM-65428): it mallocs 0x160003F, then
0x11003F for its font; the second returned NULL, and the font read into
address 0 never finished. The GS callback test now checks the new pool range.
